|~ The language: its topic, `::qlang`, whose page is the first screen a
   session reads and whose members are its chapters, and its kinds, each
   with its page and its declaration; the verbs, refusals and members of
   a kind are declared under it, `:number/add`, in the source of that
   kind. ~|

:qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline language over values. A query is a subject and its steps: each step is a command on the value the step before it answered, and answers a new value; nothing changes in place, and the answer is the last value. Everything the language handles is a value with a literal that prints and reads back as itself: data, code, prose and failures alike. So the language describes itself in its own values: every name has a page, the pages grow from ::qlang, and a query reads them like any data.

      Every `~(…)` on this page is a law: a query that runs and answers true. A form copied from a law is a form that works.

      What a reader can rely on, the invariants every rule below follows from:
      - A query is a function of its input: the same subject answers the same value, nothing changes in place, and nothing is read or written outside it but by the verbs of the host.
      - The value in the pipe is the one implicit argument: every command takes it as its subject, and every modifier is computed against it.
      - Every nested piece, an element of a literal, a group, the body of `*`, a modifier, starts from the value in the pipe of its step and the names around it and gives back only its value.
      - Nothing is converted behind the reader: no truthiness, no number read out of a string; a part read strictly, `/k` or `/0`, fails where it is missing, and one read softly, `at`, `first`, `last`, `min`, `max` or `avg`, answers null; a value that does not fit makes its step fail, under a tag naming that step.
      - One order ranks every value, so anything sorts, compares and dedupes.
      - A failure is a value: it rides past `|` and `*` until `!|` opens it.
      - Every value prints as a literal that reads back as itself: what an answer shows is the value.
      - What exists is what the language lists: a verb missing from the list of a kind does not exist for it, and a page tells the truth about its name because its laws run.

      1. The combinators. Steps are joined by combinators, each set off by spaces on both sides; they bind alike, from left to right.
      `a | b` runs b on the value a answered.
      `a * b` runs b on each element of a: a vector or a set answers the vector of the results, a map answers a map with the same keys. Several steps after `*` stand in a group, and a pipe written before `*` or `!|` stands for it.
      `a !| b` runs b only when a answered a failure; any other value passes it untouched.
      `(…)` is a group: its steps run on the value in the pipe and answer their result.
      At the top level a line is a step, joined to the next line by `|`.
      ~([1 2 3] * (mul 2 | add 1) | eq [3 5 7])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} * (mul 10) | eq {:a 10 :b 20})
      ~([1 "x" 3] * (mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0 6])
      ~(5 | (add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)

      2. The literals. A literal is a step: it replaces the value in the pipe, and every word inside it is computed against that value.
      42, -1.5 and 1e3 are numbers, finite doubles. "text" is a string. :name and :"two words" are keywords, names that stand for themselves. null, true and false. [1 2 3] is a vector, [1, 2, 3] the same vector. {:k 1} is a map, and {"k": 1} the same map: every key of a map is a keyword. #[3 1 2] is a set. `~(add 1)` is code, a quote. |~~ text ~~| is prose, a doc. !{:reason :x} is a failure. ::Box is a tag name, a value of its own. ::Box{:a 1} is a value under the tag ::Box.
      ~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])
      ~({"a": 1, "b": [true, null]} | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})
      ~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)
      A literal is how data is reshaped: write the shape the answer takes, with its parts read from the value.
      ~({:first "Ann" :age 30} | {:who /first :next (/age | add 1)} | eq {:who "Ann" :next 31})
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [:a :b]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])

      3. Reading parts. /k reads a key of a map, /0 an element of a vector, /-1 counts from the end, /a/b walks a path, / alone is the whole value. A part that is not there is a failure; `at` reads softly and answers null, and `coalesce` puts a fallback in its place, `at :k | coalesce 0`.
      ~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | /a/b/-1 | eq 20)
      ~({"first name": "Ann"} | /"first name" | eq "Ann")
      ~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)
      ~({:a 1} | at :b | eq null)
      ~({:a 1} | at :b | coalesce 0 | eq 0)

      4. Commands. A command is a name and its modifiers, one word each, separated by spaces: `add 1`, `split ","`, `filter ~(gt 1)`. A step sees one value, the one the step before handed it, and everything inside the step is computed from that value: what an earlier step saw is gone unless a name keeps it. So `mulOf /price /qty` multiplies two fields of one record, but after `/active |` a modifier reads the boolean, not the record; `:r /` keeps the record for the steps after. A command with modifiers, or a pipeline, inside a literal or a modifier stands in parentheses.
      ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)
      ~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)
      ~({:a 1} | [(/a | add 1) /a] | eq [2 1])
      ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | /qty | mul /price !| type | eq ::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError)
      ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | :r / | /qty | mul (r | /price) | eq 6)
      A modifier that is code, which a verb runs later on each element, is a quote: `~(…)`, or `~name` for one word. A value computed at the call is refused there.
      ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | count | eq 1)
      ~([{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~/n | first | eq {:n 1})
      ~([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| type | eq ::any/filter/predicate/HoldsNotMetError)

      5. Kinds and verbs. Every value has a kind, which `type` answers as a tag: ::number ::string ::keyword ::boolean ::null ::vec ::map ::set, and ::quote ::doc ::tag and others. A verb lives on the kinds it serves, and a name finds its verb through the kind of its subject; what a kind answers is listed by `::string | manifest`, and a verb outside its list does not exist for it. The verbs most queries use:
      numbers: add sub mul div round, and gt gte lt lte to compare.
      strings: split lines trim contains startsWith endsWith lower upper append prepend keyword, parseJson to read a number or any JSON out of text, and count take drop by characters; strings join from a vector by `join ","`.
      containers: count first last at take drop filter every any sort reverse distinct flat sum avg min max reduce groupBy indexBy has contains, and for maps keys vals entries; union minus inter for sets and maps. The greatest by a key is `max ~(key)`, and `sort ~([(/n | desc) /name])` ranks by n descending and then by name, and `flat` splices one level of nesting.
      every value: type eq ne print json if cond coalesce apply tag desc, and `payload` opens a value under a tag.
      booleans: and or not.
      ~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] * type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
      ~("a,b,c" | split "," | join "-" | eq "a-b-c")
      ~("db_host" | [count (drop 3) (take 2)] | eq [7 "host" "db"])
      ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
      ~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries * (unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])
      ~({:a 1} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})
      ~(3.14159 | round 2 | ne 3.14159)
      ~([3 1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)

      6. What differs from the languages a reader knows.
      One order ranks every value: null, then booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, tag names, vectors, sets, maps; so anything sorts. A set is the vector in that order without duplicates: `distinct` and `keys` answer a set, `take`, `drop` and `filter` keep a set, and `sort` and `reverse` answer a vector.
      ~(#[:z :x :y] | first | eq :x)
      ~([2 3 2 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~({:y 1 :x 2} | keys | eq #[:x :y])
      ~(#[:z :x :y] | reverse | eq [:z :y :x])
      A map's elements are its values: `count`, `sum`, `first`, `filter`, `sort` and `*` read the values, and the keys travel with them.
      ~({:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2) | eq {:b 5})
      A condition is a boolean: `if`, `filter`, `every`, `any` and `cond` refuse any other value, so nothing is truthy.
      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg") | eq "pos")
      ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| type | eq ::any/if/condition/HoldsNotMetError)
      A string and a keyword turn into each other by `keyword`; text and code turn into each other by `parse`; `print` answers the text a value reads back from, a string.
      ~("draft" | keyword | eq :draft)
      ~(:draft | keyword | eq "draft")
      ~(7 | print | eq "7")
      A name, `:n v`, holds v for the steps after it, and the value in the pipe goes on unchanged; `:n /` names the value in the pipe. A name is declared once in a scope, and the names a group declares close with it.
      ~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
      ~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)

      7. Failures. A step that fails answers a failure, a value: `|` and `*` pass it by, and `!|` opens it to its facts. The tag of a failure is the address of what refused: ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError is `add` refusing its subject for not being a number, ::number/add/addend/HoldsNotMetError its part `addend` breaking a law of its page. `!| explain` shows the page of what refused.
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| /actualType | eq ::string)
      ~(1 | add "x" !| type | eq ::number/add/addend/HoldsNotMetError)
      ~(!{:reason :late} !| /reason | eq :late)
      A failure is built from a map by `raise`, or written as a literal with the tag of its own kind before the bang; `!|` opens it to that map, its tag stacked over the kind ::error. Whether a value is a failure is `false !| true`, and `/trail` holds the path it took, the steps it skipped last.
      ~({:reason :late} | raise !| [type /reason] | eq [::error :late])
      ~(::Late!{:by 3} !| [type /by] | eq [::Late 3])
      ~([!{:k 1} 5] * (false !| true) | eq [true false])
      ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | eq ~(mul 2))

      8. Code and names of your own. A quote holds steps unrun: it is the vector of its steps, `apply` runs it on a subject, and `parse` turns text into a quote and a quote into its text.
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
      ~(5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
      ~("add 1" | parse | eq ~(add 1))
      A kind of your own is a label, a name declared by its page, whose laws, `::holds~(…)`, every value put under it keeps. A name under a label, a quote, runs on its values when mentioned. What a call gives is its object, `::Box/put[7]`, and each name under the call's address reads a part of it, `~(/0)` given or `~(at 0 d)` d by default, checked by the laws of its page and named in the body. A noun of the language takes names alike; a quote in a bare name runs by `apply`.
      ~(:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| | :Box/put ~(within ~(append w)) | :Box/put/w |~~ A weight. ::holds~(isA ::number) ~~| ~(/0) | ::Box[1] | [(put 7) (put "x" !| type)] | eq [::Box[1 7] ::Box/put/w/HoldsNotMetError])
      ~(:number/plus ~(add by) | :number/plus/by ~(at 0 1) | 5 | [plus (plus 2)] | eq [6 7])
      ~(:q ~(add 1) | 5 | apply q | eq 6)
      A step of a quote is a record of what it computes, a command under ::call with its `:name`:
      ~(~(add 1) | first | [type /name] | eq [::call :add])
      Prose is a value too: |~~ text ~~| is a doc, whose `content` is its text, `segments` its prose and quotes, and `laws` the quotes that run. A comment, written |~ like this ~| or from |~| to the end of its line, is whitespace.
      ~(|~~ a ~(eq 1) b ~~| | [(segments | count) (laws | count)] | eq [3 1])

      9. Working a task. Look at the input before shaping it: `type`, `keys`, `/0`, `count`. Write the shape of the answer as a literal, and build the query one step at a time, running each. When a step fails, its tag is the address of what refused; `!| explain` reads its page, and `::vec/sort | doc` the page of a verb, with laws to copy.
      ~([{:id 1 :tags ["a"]}] | /0 | keys | eq #[:id :tags])

      10. Asking. `x | doc` answers the page of a name or of the kind of a value: its text is `content`, its laws `laws`, its links `links`. ::snippet~(env | keys) lists the names in scope, ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) the names under a kind by address, `::vec/sort | doc` the page of one, `::any/sort | doc` the page every `sort` shares and `::any/sort | manifest` the parts of its object, and `::vec/sort | runLaws` runs its laws. `x !| explain` shows the page of what refused. An answer prints whole; `x | elide 4000` bounds it, each part left out an ::elision whose `:read` continues the query to it.
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | count | gt 5)

      11. The chapters, in the order they rest on each other, each naming its neighbours and its kinds:
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang/names | doc) names and their records;
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc) the verbs that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc) the pages and how to move through them;
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc) the tool the language runs in and the budget of an answer.
  ~~|

:builtin
  |~~ The descriptor, a map of fields under this tag. A host's primitive
      is a name under a label whose value is an empty one, `::builtin{}`,
      and a kind a host declares ends with one: it runs the implementation
      the host handed for its name, `::number/add` and `::vec` the
      language's own, while a name written in steps is a quote.
      A descriptor is data and runs nothing by itself: a bare name bound
      to one reads it, and in a verb it is a step.

      ~(::builtin{} | :c / | [1 2] | c | type | eq ::builtin)
      ~(::number/add | spec | eq ::builtin{})
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:verb
  |~~ A verb, a quote under this tag: its leading declarations are its
      signature and the rest its body. A reader's verb lives on the kind
      its name is declared under, while the catalog is written as names
      under labels, each with the parts of its object, which a reader
      declares alike. A declaration whose body is a tag name is a slot the
      call fills, one whose body is a set of kinds takes a value of any of
      them and may be left out when `::null` is among them, and one whose
      body is any other literal takes that literal when it is left out;
      `* :name kind` gathers the modifiers that remain. `:subject` and
      `:returns` are the roles, the kind the verb serves and the kind it
      answers, `/` for its subject's own. A name bound to a verb runs it
      when it is mentioned, each modifier evaluated at the call and
      checked by the kind of its slot, and `~name` hands it on as code.
      ::link~(::spec | doc) ::link~(::quote | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      ~(:double ::verb~(mul 2) | 3 | double | double | eq 12)
      ~(:least ::verb~(:n 1 | sort | take n) | [4 8 6] | [least (least 2)] | eq [[4] [4 6]])
      ~(:scale ::verb~(:by ::number | mul by) | [1 2] * scale / | eq [1 4])
      ~(:big ::verb~(gt 2) | [1 2 3 4] | filter ~big | eq [3 4])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:tag
  |~~ The kind of a tag name, `::Kind` taken as a value. A tag is the name
      of a kind, the interpretation of the value it stands on, and the
      model of the language hangs on it: a value carries its kind as the
      tag over it, a name finds its verb through that tag, and the tag
      names the page of the kind, so `type | doc` reads the page of a
      value's kind. Tags stack over any payload and read from the outside
      in; behaviour does not travel with a value, it is found again
      through the tag wherever the value goes. A kind is a label, a name
      declared by its page, and the tag is the address of that name, so a
      page declared as `:d` makes the kind `::d`; a tag no node answers is
      a kind of identity, which declares nothing.
      ::snippet~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type) answers `::Box`, and
      ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(::Box | type | eq ::tag)
      ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ::holds~(gt 0) ~~| | ::Pos(-3) !| type | eq ::Pos/HoldsNotMetError)
      ~(::tag::Box | eq ::Box)
      ~(5 | type | doc | type | eq ::doc)
      ~(:d |~~ A d. ~~| | :d/construct ~({:s /}) | ::d(10) | [type payload] | eq [::d {:s 10}])
      ~(::Unbound[1] | type | spec !| type | eq ::any/SpecBindingNotFoundError)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:null
  |~~ The kind of null, the absence of a value; a part a call may leave
      out reads null, `~(at 0)`. Its constructor, as every
      constructor of a kind of the core, reads a payload of the kind as
      the value itself. ::link~(::any/at | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(null | type | eq ::null)
      ~(::null(null) | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:boolean
  |~~ The kind of `true` and `false`, the answer of a question about a
      value: `filter`, `every` and `if` take a quote that answers one,
      and `false !| true` after steps answers whether they failed.
      ::snippet~(::boolean | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(true | type | eq ::boolean)
      ~(::boolean(false) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:number
  |~~ The kind of a number, a finite double: an infinity or NaN is no
      value, so a step whose result leaves the domain answers an error.
      No kind reads text, so `::number(5)` is `5` and `::number"5"` is
      refused; `parseJson` reads a number out of a string. Its literal
      is JSON's, a leading zero allowed, and its precision the double's own.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(5 | type | eq ::number)
      A literal past the range is no number the parser reads:
      ~("1e400" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)
      A step whose result leaves the range answers an error:
      ~(1e308 | mul 10 !| type | eq ::number/mul/ResultNotFiniteError)
      An integer and a decimal are one kind, rounded as a double rounds:
      ~([-1 2.5e-3 (0.1 | add 0.2)] | eq [-1 0.0025 0.30000000000000004])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:string
  |~~ The kind of a string, text as it came. A JSON string arrives as a
      string, and `keyword` makes one a keyword where a key is wanted,
      since the keys of a map are keywords. Its verbs work on the text
      whole, by its characters, `count`, `take` and `drop`, or by its
      lines, and `lines` breaks it into the vector a pipeline reads in
      pieces. Its literal is written in double quotes
      with the escapes of JSON, any character raw between them, a line
      break among them.
      ::snippet~(::string | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::keyword | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~("x" | type | eq ::string)
      ~(::string"x" | eq "x")
      The escapes of JSON, `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\t` and `\uXXXX` among them:
      ~("A\t\"" | eq "A	\"")
      ~("A" | eq "A")
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:keyword
  |~~ The kind of a keyword, `:k`, a name that stands for itself. It is
      apart from the string so that no name is read by its look: every
      key of a map is a keyword, `/k` reads a field by it, and a keyword
      names a binding of the scope; `keyword` makes one of a string and
      a string of one. A bare keyword is a name that begins with a letter
      of any script, `_` or `@` and goes on with letters, digits, `_` and
      `-`; a slash joins names into a path, `:domain/user`;
      any other text is written in quotes, `:"foo bar"`, and the quotes
      around a bare name change nothing. ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~(::keyword:k | eq :k)
      The three forms, and the quoted form of a bare name:
      ~([:данные :domain/user :"$ref"] * keyword | eq ["данные" "domain/user" "$ref"])
      ~(:"foo" | eq :foo)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:vec
  |~~ The kind of a vector, the value of a `[…]` literal, and the shape a
      long value takes: its parts have an index, a size and a slice,
      `drop` and `take`, so the rest of a long answer is the same query
      with a tail, and `*` runs a step on each element. A set, a quote
      and a doc are vectors under their tags, read as vectors wherever a
      vector is read; over them the constructor answers the vector of
      their elements, and `::vec[1 2]` reads as `[1 2]`. A JSON array is
      a vector literal too, a comma between every two elements; one literal
      is written in one of the two forms, as a map's is.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::each | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
      ~(#[2 1] | tag ::vec | eq [1 2])
      ~([1, 2] | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:set
  |~~ The set, the vector in the one order without duplicates, the value
      of a `#[…]` literal, of `distinct` and of `keys`; `has` asks it
      for membership. A set is read as a vector wherever a vector is
      read; `filter`, `take`, `drop` and `flat` keep it, since its
      constructor re-runs after them, and an operand that imposes an
      order, `sort` or `reverse`, answers a vector. `*` over a set answers
      the vector of its images, one per element, and `distinct` after it
      makes a set of them again.
      ::link~(::vec | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(#[3 1 3] | eq #[1 3])
      ~([3 1 3] | tag ::set | eq ([3 1 3] | distinct))
      ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
      ~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
      A set written with braces is refused with the brackets it takes:
      ~("#{1}" | parse !| /message | contains "#[")
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:map
  |~~ The kind of a map, the value of a `{…}` literal: named facts under
      keywords, the shape a JSON object arrives in. A map literal is also
      the shape an answer takes, each value a group run on the subject,
      `{:id /id :n (/tags | count)}`; `/k` reads a field, and `*` over
      a map runs a step on each value under its key. A JSON object is a
      map literal too, its string keys read as keywords, so JSON pastes
      into a query as it is; one literal is written in one of the two
      forms, and a key written twice holds its last value. A map comes
      apart into its pairs `[key value]` by `entries` and back by
      `tag ::map`, which reads a key written as a string or a number as
      the keyword of its text.
      ::snippet~(::map | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~({:a 1} | type | eq ::map)
      ~(::map{:a 1} | eq {:a 1})
      ~({"a": 1, "b": [true, null]} | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})
      ~({:a 1 :a 2} | eq {:a 2})
      ~([[:a 1] ["b" 2]] | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:quote
  |~~ Code as a value, the value of a `~(…)` literal: a vector of steps
      under the code tag. Code is a value so that a name takes code as a
      part of its object, `filter`, `sort`, `if`; a page holds laws that run and links
      that lead on; the trail of an error holds the steps it skipped,
      ready to replay; and a query is read, printed and built like any
      data. A literal step is the literal itself; a step that computes is
      a record, `::call`, `::proj`, `::bind` or `::construct`; `*`, `!|`
      and parentheses wrap their step as `::each`, `::fail` and
      `::group`. `apply` runs a quote on the subject and `parse` prints
      it back. ::link~(::each | doc) ::link~(::call | doc)
      ::link~(::verb | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)

      ~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)
      ~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(/name | eq :mul) | parse | eq "mul 2")
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:doc
  |~~ The kind of a doc, prose as a value, the value of a doc literal:
      text that carries quotes of qlang. It is how the language says what
      it is: every page is a doc, `doc` answers the page of a name, and
      because a doc is a value its code is checked and the next step can
      take it apart. A quote with no tag is a law of the page, which
      `runLaws` holds to answer true; a quote under `::link` is a
      link, a query a reader follows. `content` answers its text,
      `segments` its parts, `laws` its laws, `links` its links and
      `snippets` its snippets.
      ::snippet~(::doc | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::link | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)

      ~(::number/add | doc | type | eq ::doc)
      ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)
      ~(["a" ~(add 1)] | tag ::doc | eq (|~~a~(add 1)~~|))
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:spec
  |~~ The signature of a reader's verb, its leading declarations as a
      quote under this tag with its subject filled, which `spec` answers
      for a verb: what a verb takes and answers is known before it is
      called. `spec` of a name under a label answers its value, a quote
      or a `::builtin{}`, and `manifest` the parts it declares, that of
      the contract of its word the parts its kinds share.
      ::snippet~(:m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | :m | spec) shows one.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      ~(:m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | :m | spec | type | eq ::spec)
      ~(:m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | :m | spec | payload * /name | eq [:subject :x])
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:binding
  |~~ The kind of the record a declaration writes into its scope. A name
      is a record that carries its page and its source, so what is named
      can be listed and asked: `:name`, the keyword it binds, `:docs`,
      one doc per doc of its slot, `:value`, `:source`, the quote of the
      declaring step, and `:origin`, the source or the cell it was read
      from. `env` answers the records of the scope, a name reads the value
      its record holds, and the axes project the record, so `env | /x |
      doc` answers the page of `x`. A value a host binds is a binding
      without a doc and without a source. ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /value | eq 1)
      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| 1 | env | /x | doc | content | eq " One. ")
      ~(:x 1 | env | /x | /name | eq :x)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:error
  |~~ The kind of errors. A reader learning a tool fails often, and
      a failure is the moment when the knowledge it needs is cheapest to
      give, so a failure is a value that carries it: the facts of the
      failure, the path it took, and the tag of the site that refused,
      which names the page of the procedure. An error is a map of facts
      under `::error`, `::error{:k 1}`, a value like any other until it
      is raised, and the bang of a literal raises it, `!{:k 1}`. Every
      refusal is a tag of its own, named for what failed,
      `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, stacked over the error. `|` passes a
      raised error by, `*` stops on one, and `!|` opens it to that
      stack, so `!| type` reads the site, the verbs of this kind reach
      every error, and every step works on it as on any value: a fact is
      added and the error raised again, or it is recovered by the tag of
      its site. ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

      ~(!{:detail "boom"} !| type | eq ::error)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::error | not)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)
      ~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:elision
  |~~ The kind of the marker of what a view left out. A
      reader's context only grows, and whatever an answer adds is read
      again on every later step, so a reader asks for a budget, by
      `elide` in the query or by the host's flag, and the marker keeps
      the way back; an answer nobody bounded prints whole: `:size` counts the elements, the
      entries or the characters it stands for, `:read` is the quote that
      applied to the value taken answers the part left out, and `:head`
      holds the first characters of a string. `elide` writes markers,
      and a reader continues its query by `:read` to see the rest.
      ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)

      ~(::elision | doc | type | eq ::doc)
      ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out"} | elide 20 | /s | /size | eq 32)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:desc
  |~~ The kind of a value turned to descend: among the values of this
      kind the one order runs in reverse, the greater payload first, so a
      key of `sort`, `min` or `max` under `desc` descends whatever the
      kind of what it ranks, a string among them, and a place of a vector
      key descends alone, `sort ~([(/count | desc) /name])`. Every
      comparison agrees, `gt` and `lt` here as `sort` and `max`, and
      `payload` opens the value beneath. `desc` lays the kind over any
      value. ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      ~(5 | desc | type | eq ::desc)
      ~(5 | desc | payload | eq 5)
      ~(::desc(5) | eq (5 | desc))
      ~([2 1 3] * desc | sort | * payload | eq [3 2 1])
      ~(["b" "c" "a"] | sort ~(desc) | eq ["c" "b" "a"])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:link
  |~~ The kind of a link, a query a page names for its reader to follow,
      written in the prose of a doc as a quote under `::link`.
      Links are what make the pages a hypertext: a page names the pages
      that go deeper, and a reader reaches them by `open`, which runs the
      query the link holds. A quote with no tag is a law, which answers
      true; a link opens a page, so `links * open` answers pages alone,
      and a query that lists or shows something is a snippet. The suite
      follows every link of the catalog, so none goes dead. A host may
      stack its own tag over a link.
      ::snippet~(::qlang | doc | links) lists the links of the first page.
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)

      ~(|~~ ::link~(::qlang/values | doc) ~~| | links | first | type | eq ::link)
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | every ~(type | eq ::doc))
      ~(|~~ ::Host::link~(1) ~~| | links | count | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:snippet
  |~~ The kind of a snippet, a query a page shows its reader to run and
      see, written in the prose of a doc as a quote under `::snippet`: a
      listing, ::snippet~(::vec | manifest), or a demonstration of
      a form, ::snippet~({:a [5 6]} | /a/-1). A law answers true and a
      link opens a page; a snippet answers whatever it shows, which
      `open` runs, and `snippets` reads the snippets of a doc. The suite
      runs every snippet of the catalog, so none answers an error.
      ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)

      ~(|~~ ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | snippets | first | open | has ::vec/count)
      ~(::vec | doc | snippets | count | gt 0)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:explanation
  |~~ The kind of what `explain` answers: the page `doc` reads
      for a value under `:page`, then the value itself under `:value`, so
      it prints as the page above what it explains, an error
      raised, with its bang. It is the cord from a value to its
      procedure in one answer; `page` and `value` answer its parts.
      ::link~(::error | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | type | eq ::explanation)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | value !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:tagged
  |~~ The kind of every value under a tag, `::Box[1 2]`, `::Box{:a 1}`,
      whatever the payload: the walk from a tagged value to its verb
      passes its own tags, then this kind, then the kind of its payload's
      shape. The verbs that take a tag apart live here, `payload`, which
      answers the value beneath, and `within`, which edits it and puts
      the tag back through its constructor. A value is put under a tag
      by a literal, `::T<payload>`, a step of its own kind, or by `tag`.
      ::link~(::tag | doc) ::link~(::construct | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)

      Every value under a tag reaches the verbs of this kind:
      ~(:Box ::spec~() | ::Box[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
      The step that builds one is no value of this kind:
      ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | type | eq ::construct)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:construct
  |~~ The step of a constructor invocation, `::T<payload>`: it puts a
      payload under the tag `::T` through the tag's constructor, which
      checks or builds the value. As a record, `:tag` is the tag and
      `:payload` the step of its payload. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:call
  |~~ The step of a command, `verb object`: the name finds its verb
      through the kind of the subject and runs it with the modifiers.
      As a record, `:name` is the keyword of the name it calls and
      `:args` the quote of each argument's pipeline when the call has
      parentheses. Like every record, a `::call` is the step its own text
      reads back as: the constructor checks the fields against the schema,
      prints the step and reads it back. ::link~(::quote | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~(filter ~(gt 1)) | payload | eq [::call{:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]}])
      ~(~(count) | first | /name | eq :count)
      ~({:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(filter ~(gt 1)))
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:proj
  |~~ The step of a projection, `/a/0/b`: it reads a part of the value in
      the pipe by keys and indices, a negative index from the end, and a
      miss answers an error, never null, so a misspelled field is found
      where it is read. `/` is the value itself. A key is written as a
      keyword is, bare, in quotes, `/"foo bar"`, or with its colon when it
      is a path, `/:qlang/error`, which is one key where `/qlang/error`
      is two. As a record, `:path` is the keys and indices it walks,
      `[]` for `/`.
      ::snippet~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | /a/b/-1) answers `6`.
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~(/items/0/name) | first | /path | eq [:items 0 :name])
      ~(~(/) | first | /path | eq [])
      ~({:"foo bar" {:qlang/error 1}} | /"foo bar"/:qlang/error | eq 1)
      ~({:qlang {:error 2}} | /qlang/error | eq 2)
      ~(5 | mul / | eq 25)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:bind
  |~~ The step of a declaration, `:name body`: it names a value for the
      steps after it, in the scope where it stands, and a group, an
      element of a literal and the body of `*` each open a scope of their
      own, so a name never leaks out of the fork that declared it. As a
      record, `:name` is the keyword or tag it binds, `:docs` its prose
      and `:body` the step of its body. ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~(:inc add 1) | first | [/name /body/name] | eq [:inc :add])
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:each
  |~~ The step `* body`, the distribute: it runs its body on each
      element of a vector or each value of a map and answers the results
      in their place, the map of other languages; the first element whose
      body fails is its answer, the elements after it left unrun,
      and a body that recovers, `* (f !| 0)`, keeps every element. `*`
      stands in the place of `|`, and several steps of a body stand in
      parentheses. As a record it wraps the quote of its body.
      ::link~(::vec | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~(* add 1) | first | payload | eq ~(add 1))
      ~(~(* (add 1 | mul 2)) | first | payload | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:fail
  |~~ The step `!| step`, the fail track: it runs its step on an error
      alone, opened to its facts, and passes any other value by, so it is
      where a failure is read, recovered or raised again, while `|`
      passes the error by. As a record it wraps the quote of that one
      step. ::link~(::error | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

      ~(~(!| /kind) | first | payload | eq ~(/kind))
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:group
  |~~ The step `(pipeline)`, a group: it runs its steps on the value in
      the pipe and answers their result, its names left inside. Each part
      of a literal is such a group, which is why a literal is the shape an
      answer takes, `[(add 1) (mul 2)]`. As a record it wraps the quote
      of its pipeline. ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      ~(~((add 1 | mul 2)) | first | payload | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::spec~(::builtin{})

:any
  |~~ The kind beneath every kind, where the verbs every value answers
      live: `type`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `explain`, `elide` and
      their kin. A verb that means the same for any value is written once
      here, and the walk from a name to its verb ends here, after the
      kinds of the subject. No value answers it as its `type`, since each
      has a kind of its own above it.
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) lists the verbs of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      ~(::any | manifest | has ::any/type)
      ~(5 | type | eq ::any | not)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:qlang/values
  |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, data, code, prose and
      failures alike, so everything the language says about itself is a
      value the pipeline reads. A value never changes, and each has a
      kind, the name of which is a tag: it tells how to read the value it
      stands on, and the tag taken as a value, `::Box`, is the kind
      itself, which a query asks for its page and its verbs. Rests on
      nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and the hypertext rest on it.
      ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
      ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)

      A literal implies its kind:
      ~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] * type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
      A tag written before a payload puts it under the tag, whatever
      brackets the payload takes, `::T{…}`, `::T[…]`, `::T(…)` or `::T"…"`:
      ~([::Box{:a 1} ::Box[1 2] ::Box(1 | add 2) ::Box"s"] * payload | eq [{:a 1} [1 2] 3 "s"])
      `tag` puts the value in the pipe under a tag, tags stack and read
      from the outside in, and `payload` takes one off:
      ~([7 8] | tag ::Inner | tag ::Outer | [type (payload | type) (payload | payload)] | eq [::Outer ::Inner [7 8]])
      ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)
      A step answers a new value and the old one stays as it was:
      ~([1 2] | :v / | append 3 | [v /] | eq [[1 2] [1 2 3]])
      Every value prints as a literal that reads back as itself:
      ~({:a [1 #[2]] :t ::Box[3]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)
  ~~|
  #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set]

:qlang/pipeline
  |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps, each a command
      on the value the step before it answered, and the answer is the
      last value; the shape of an answer is written as the literal it
      takes, so a query names what it reads. Rests on values; the verbs
      are its commands, and code holds its steps as a value.
      ::link~(::each | doc) ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::group | doc) ::link~(::qlang/names | doc)

      `|` runs the next step on the answer of the one before:
      ~(5 | add 1 | mul 2 | eq 12)
      A literal is a step that replaces the value in the pipe, a tag name
      among them, so a value goes under a tag by `tag`:
      ~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])
      ~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)
      `*` stands in the place of `|` and runs its step on each element of
      a vector or each value of a map, a literal among its steps:
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [1 2]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])
      A pipe written before `*` or `!|` stands for the combinator after it:
      ~([1 2] | * (add 1) | eq [2 3])
      `/k`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/a/b` read a part, and a miss is an error:
      ~({:a {:b 1}} | /a/b | eq 1)
      ~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)
      A modifier is computed against the subject of its own step, the
      value the step before it answered, so two fields of one record are
      read in one step, and a name keeps the record for a later step:
      ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | mulOf /qty /price | eq 6)
      ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | :r / | /qty | mul (r | /price) | eq 6)
      A name holds the value in the pipe for the steps after it, and
      steps in parentheses run on the value in the pipe and answer their
      result:
      ~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
      At the top level a line is a step, joined to the next by `|` unless
      one of them carries its combinator, and inside brackets a line
      break is a space; a comment, `|~ … ~|`, nesting, or `|~|` to the
      end of its line, is whitespace wherever a space stands:
      ~("[3 1 2]\n|~| the order\nsort\n| first" | parse | apply / | eq 1)
      ~("|~ outer |~ inner ~| ~| [1 2] |~ a note ~| | count" | parse | apply / | eq 2)
      `|`, `*` and `!|` bind alike, from left to right. Each part of a
      literal, a group and the body of `*` is a fork: it starts from the
      value and the names outside it, and only its answer leaves it, so
      the parts of one literal see nothing of each other:
      ~(:n 10 | [1 2] * (add n) | eq [11 12])
      ~([(:a 1 | a) (a)] | /1 !| type | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)
  ~~|
  #[::each ::proj ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]

:qlang/names
  |~~ Names. A name holds a value for the steps after it, the one way a
      query remembers: `:n v` declares it in the scope where it stands,
      and a verb and a kind are names alike. A name with a path declares
      a node under the noun its path names before its last name,
      `:Box/area` under `::Box`, a noun declared before it, and the tag
      of a name is its address, `::Box/area`. A name is a record,
      `::binding`, with its page, its source, its value and the source
      it came from, so where a thing was declared is read as data. The
      scope of a step is what the steps before it wrote; a step inside
      `(…)` or `*` writes a scope that closes with it. Under
      every scope lies the root, which the host's sources declared as
      cells before the first step of a reader, the language's first; a
      query, a cell of a session and a source of the root are written
      alike. Rests on values and the pipeline; the verbs are names that
      run. ::link~(::binding | doc) ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)

      `env` lists what the reader declared, `kinds` the kinds the scope
      reads, and what lies under a noun `manifest` lists:
      ~(:limit 10 | env | has :limit)
      ~(kinds | has ::vec)
      ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/count)
      A name reads its record by `binding`:
      ~(:x 1 | :x | binding | /value | eq 1)
      ~(::vec/filter | binding | /origin | eq :qlang/vec)
      A name declared inside a group closes with it:
      ~(1 | (:inner 2 | inner) | env | has :inner | not)
      A name declared under a label resides on it: it serves the values
      of the label, found by its name through the walk of their tags,
      and refuses any other subject under its address; a quote taken
      from one label and declared under another resides on the second:
      ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/twice ~(/w | mul 2) | ::Box{:w 3} | twice | eq 6)
      ~(:vec/second ~(/1) | [7 8 9] | second | eq 8)
      ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/w2 ~(/w | mul 2) | {:w 3} | Box/w2 !| type | eq ::Box/w2/SubjectNotBoxError)
      ~(:A |~~ A. ~~| | :A/f ~(1) | :B |~~ B. ~~| | :B/g (::A/f | binding | /value) | [(::B{} | g) (::A{} | B/g !| type)] | eq [1 ::B/g/SubjectNotBError])
      ~(:Box/area ~(1) !| type | eq ::bind/NounNotDeclaredError)
      A name is declared once in a scope and again in a scope inside it,
      which shadows it for the steps after; a value the reader names
      takes no word of the vocabulary, the name of a verb or of a name
      under a label, so no step meets it where the subject's word was
      meant; an address the root holds is declared once at all, so a
      reader extends a noun of the root and replaces none of its nodes:
      ~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)
      ~(:k 1 | (:k 2 | k) | eq 2)
      ~(:count 5 !| type | eq ::bind/NameTakenByVocabularyError)
      ~(:vec/count ~(0) !| type | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)
      A name begins with a letter of any script, `_` or `@`; `@` marks a
      name whose call reaches outside the language, and a name whose body
      calls one carries it too, while a name over the value a call
      answered is plain:
      ~(:any/@io ~(1) | :fine (@io) | fine | eq 1)
      ~(:any/quiet ~(@out) !| type | eq ::bind/EffectMarkMissingError)
      The docs in the slot of a declaration, between its name and its
      body, are its page, one doc each:
      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| |~~ Two. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /docs | count | eq 2)
      A name under a label resolves its names through the tree of names
      as it stands at the call, itself among them, so it recurses by
      name, and the frames of a runaway meet a budget:
      ~(:map/walk ~({:n /n :kids (/kids * walk)}) | {:n 1 :kids [{:n 2 :kids []}]} | walk | /kids/0/n | eq 2)
      ~(:number/inf ~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf !| type | eq ::call/EvaluationDepthExceededError)
  ~~|
  #[::binding]

:qlang/verbs
  |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves, and
      has an address, `::vec/sort`; so what can be done with a value is
      asked of its kind, and one word serves many kinds. The verbs of a
      kind and those of every value are the whole of them: a verb in
      neither list does not exist. Rests on values and the pipeline;
      errors name the verb that refused.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists the verbs of a kind and
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) those of every value.
      ::link~(::verb | doc) ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)

      A name finds its verb through the kind of its subject: its tags
      from the outside in, then the shape beneath them, then the verbs
      every value answers, on `::any`; a verb of vectors reaches a
      vector under a tag and keeps the tag:
      ~([3 1 2] | sort | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(::Box[3 1 2] | sort | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
      A name declares the parts of its object as names under it, and a
      word several kinds answer declares once, under its contract on
      `::any`, the parts its kinds share, which a kind's primitive reads at
      each place it reads no part of its own; `manifest` answers them
      before a call:
      ~(::map/at | manifest | eq #[::map/at/key])
      ~(::any/at | manifest | eq #[::any/at/default ::any/at/index])
      A reader's name under a noun takes the parts of its object after
      it, each a name under the call's address:
      ~(:number/plus ~(add by) | :number/plus/by |~~ How much. ::holds~(isA ::number) ~~| ~(/0) | 5 | plus 2 | eq 7)
      A modifier is evaluated at the call against the subject, the value
      in the pipe, which is always the subject of the call; a form that
      combines several values of one subject is a verb of its own, its
      objects all the operands, `mulOf /price /qty`; a part that runs
      code later takes a quote, `~name` or `~/path` the quote of one step, and a
      condition is a boolean:
      ~({:price 100 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 300)
      ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gt 18) | count | eq 1)
      ~([{:age 3}] | sort ~/age | first | /age | eq 3)
      ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| type | eq ::any/FilterConditionNotBooleanError)
      A kind is a label, a name declared by its page, whose laws every
      value put under it keeps, and whose name `construct`, when it has
      one, builds the value from the payload:
      ~(:Small |~~ Below ten. ::holds~(lt 10) ~~| | [(::Small(3) | type) (::Small(12) !| type)] | eq [::Small ::Small/HoldsNotMetError])
      ~(:d |~~ A d. ~~| | :d/construct ~({:s /}) | ::d(10) | [type payload] | eq [::d {:s 10}])
      A tag no declaration answers is a kind of identity, which checks
      nothing and lays the tag on the value:
      ~(::Small(12) | payload | eq 12)
  ~~|
  #[::verb ::spec ::any]

:qlang/errors
  |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
      error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the path it
      took and the tag of the site that refused, which names the page of
      its procedure. A reader learning a tool fails often, and the
      failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give. Rests on
      values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds the pages the
      sites name.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::explanation | doc)

      A failed step answers an error the tag of its site names:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
      `|` passes an error by and `*` answers the first an element answers:
      ~([1 "x" 3] * (mul 2) !| type | eq ::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError)
      `a !| b` runs `b` on an error alone, opened to its facts, and
      recovers each failure where it is read:
      ~([1 "x"] * (mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
      The facts name what refused, and the first stop of the trail holds
      the value the step refused:
      ~("a" | mul 2 !| [/actualType /trail/0/subject] | eq [::string "a"])
      `explain` shows the page of the site above the error:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | type | eq ::explanation)
      The trail holds a stop for each level the error left, and the last
      stop the steps it skipped, a quote ready to replay:
      ~(:any/f ~(add n | mul n) | :any/f/n ~(/0) | "x" | f 2 | sub 3 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(add n) ~(f 2)])
      ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | parse | eq "mul 2")
      A map raised again after `!|` resumes the trail it holds, and one
      without `:trail` starts it over:
      ~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| raise | add 1 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(!{:kind :oops})])
      ~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(raise)])
      Whether a value is an error is `false !| true`:
      ~([!{:kind :oops} 42] * (false !| true) | eq [true false])
  ~~|
  #[::error ::fail ::explanation]

:qlang/code
  |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb takes
      one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply` runs one on
      a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built like any vector of
      steps. Because code is a value, a page holds laws that run and
      links that open, and the trail of an error holds the steps it
      skipped, ready to replay. Rests on values and the pipeline; the
      hypertext is written in it.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)

      A quote held as a value runs by `apply`:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q / | 5 | apply q | eq 12)
      A quote is the vector of its steps:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  #[::quote]

:qlang/hypertext
  |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::` beginning
      an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes from an address
      into one of its projections, as a path reaches a file: `doc` into
      its page, `spec` into its declaration, `source` into the code that
      declared it, and `runLaws` runs the laws of its page. An address holds no one
      entity but a set of values, and each passage reads one of them; the
      address, the verb and what it reaches are all values, so the
      pipeline goes on from a projection as from any value, and a tag
      you declare with verbs on it adds projections of its own.
      A keyword after `doc` is an anchor within the page of its subject,
      `::qlang/pipeline | doc`, a vector of anchors a path, a set of
      anchors several pages at once. A page is a doc, prose that holds
      code: a quote with no tag is a law, which `runLaws` holds to
      answer true, a quote under `::link` is a link to another page, and
      a quote under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see what it
      shows; `open` runs either. So a page leads on to the pages that go deeper,
      and the pipeline moves through them as through any data:
      `manifest` lists what lies under a noun, its verbs and refusals,
      `links * open` opens every page a page names in one query, and a
      filter over pages by `content | contains` searches them. Nothing
      is read that was not asked for, and a query names the shape of
      what it reads. Rests on every concept before it.
      ::link~(::doc | doc) ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)

      The links of a page open in one query:
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | count | gt 0)
      One address passes into its page, its declaration and its source:
      ~(::vec/take | [(doc | type) (spec | type) (source | type)] | eq [::doc ::builtin ::quote])
      A filter over pages searches them:
      ~(::vec | manifest | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])
  ~~|
  #[::doc ::link ::snippet]

:qlang/hosts
  |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it and
      brings verbs of its own; the command line is one. A value is whole
      in the language, and the host prints an answer whole unless a
      reader asks for a budget, by `elide` in the query or by the host's
      flag, the command line's `--budget=N`: what a budget left out is
      an `::elision` marker, whose `:read` continues the query to the
      part. The command line reads JSON or text from stdin and writes
      its answer back in the same form. Rests on values and the hypertext.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)

      A value whose print fits its budget is answered as it is:
      ~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  #[::elision]
